# Vault entry recycle-bin candidate

This work-only candidate adds native entry recycling, recovery to an existing previous group, and confirmed permanent deletion. It copies the accepted five Vault runtime modules into a private validation layout; production and all earlier receipts remain unchanged. The application runs its own bounded implementation and never loads KeePass assemblies or launches the original program.

Normal entry deletion now moves the complete native entry into the UUID-designated recycle bin when recycling is enabled. A missing bin is created beneath the root with trash icon43, searching and Auto-Type disabled, and expanded state matching the released default collapse setting. Deletion preserves entry UUID, content, protected string flags, custom fields, attachment references, history, and other native XML metadata. It records the previous parent UUID, changes location time, and applies the released non-modifying touch behavior. Recovery moves the same entry back by UUID; it keeps content and history and records the old bin as the new previous parent. Permanent deletion removes the entry and writes a native UUID/time tombstone. Recycling-disabled imports and entries already in a bin or its descendants take that permanent path.

The client keeps bin entries out of the active entry list and provides a separate bin list, read-only masked preview, confirmed recovery and confirmed permanent purge. Active editing/history/TOTP commands refuse recycled entries. Bin preview/list/mutation publication checks view and bin generations after waits. These checks preserve another selected editor and its newer draft and suppress secrets arriving after Close bin or view cleanup. They retain the accepted history dirty-editor, pending-draft, selection and disposal protections.

`delete` remains an old API alias: a confirmed ID-only call is accepted for backward compatibility. Its missing revision does **not** receive stale-selection protection. The current client supplies a revision; new `recycleDelete`, `recycleRestore` and `recycleRead` require the private graph-bound revision. Any represented database, attachment or private-key change invalidates that revision. These tokens and enabled-field draft guards are Studio policies; they are not asserted to be KeePass API features.

## Evidence and original boundary

The final finite campaign is recorded by [record-candidate.mjs](record-candidate.mjs). It runs41 exact accepted history regression groups and27 new groups:12 native graph cases,5 encrypted in-memory server cases,4 DOM element-model UI cases,2 actual shared-host HTTP cases and4 original comparison groups. The expected total is68 with no skips. The final run receipt supplies the measured result and exact source hashes; this README does not rebind old18,41,11 or browser evidence to the candidate.

The new original corpus executes the SHA-authenticated KeePass2.61.1 assembly in14 processes. Its three lifecycle/permanence groups use a real `QualityCheck=false` feature to make the released writer select KDBX4.1. They compare represented group/entry identities, placement, touch state, history contents, protected/custom/dangerously named string fields, tags, attachments and native tombstones. The original reader opens candidate exports. An additional group separately preserves the fixture using the default feature set: the released writer selects4.0 and drops `PreviousParentGroup` on save/reopen. Candidate export explicitly writes4.1 and retains it; the original4.1 reader can read that retained reference. A subsequent original save without a4.1-only feature can downgrade and drop it again. This is a conditional4.1 comparison, not default-release lifecycle equality.

Actual original library operations include database New/Open/Save, collection Remove, AddEntry, entry CreateBackup/Touch, tombstone construction, and the released private static `EnsureRecycleBin` helper. Delete/restore orchestration and deterministic timestamp assignments are source-composed from authenticated source; the full original GUI handlers do not execute. Detailed logs keep those counts separate. The final68 campaign expects38 actual assembly loads total:24 in the unchanged prior-history corpus and14 in the new recycle corpus. Original GUI executions and full-product executions are0. It expects9 actual shared-host processes:5 prior-history processes and4 recycle processes. New client evidence uses4 owned DOM-model groups; browser executions in this stage are0. Earlier failed experiments remain separate and are not included in these final counts.

The disabled unused expiry time of a newly created bin is a precise remaining difference: the released constructor initializes it from process-global `PwDefs.DtDefaultNow`; the candidate initializes it from the operation clock. `Expires=false` in both. The original bin's unadapted expiry is retained in observations. Deterministic fixture clock assignments explicitly adapt temporal fields for comparison, so no exact original clock/event behavior is claimed.

## Bounds and open work

The slice caps preserved XML plus attachment base64 text at8MiB, entries/groups/tombstones at10,000 each, XML nodes at100,000, XML depth at64 and graph group depth at63. It checks capacity before creating another bin. Usage-count updates use bounded uint64 arithmetic. Clocks must be valid UTC values from years0001–9999 with whole seconds or three-digit fractions. Incoming API data is snapshotted before the per-workspace queue awaits, with12MiB text/key bytes,100,000 structure nodes and depth64. Imported KDF and native container bounds are inherited from the accepted KDBX implementation.

Group deletion, recursive group recovery, whole-bin emptying, multi-selection deletion, moving to arbitrary groups, bin settings, localization and UI collapse configuration are not implemented here. `deleteGroup`, `recycleGroup` and `emptyRecycleBin` explicitly reject. Previous-parent recovery is offered only for bin entries with an existing different parent outside the bin; the released general move-to-previous-group command has broader scope. A malformed root-as-bin UUID is rejected rather than accepting every released orchestration path. Unknown native fields stay preserved, but this is not complete KeePass metadata validation, synchronization or full format/product parity. Earlier history deletion/dialog policies, expiration workflows, Auto-Type execution, plugins/providers, all remaining manual features and security comparisons remain open. Full-product parity, security parity and superiority are false.

## Reproduction and licensing

Use Windows, Node24.16.0, Windows PowerShell with .NET, and the exact locally preserved KeePass2.61.1 portable assembly. Set `REA_KEEPASS_EXE` when its default lab path is unavailable. The helper verifies SHA256 `04d29636c393f0c1fc99ff739295494be3adefded93271e44591e8e300225a5a` before process launch and again before assembly load. All fixtures use synthetic passwords/data in owned temporary or stage-private folders. The copied shared host uses loopback and a private token; runtime/oracle/source paths must return404. Tests write detailed observations to `.local` and owned temporary directories, never app result directories. Capture/child deadlines bound ordinary child operations; hard fingerprint-I/O deadlines and race-free Windows descendant isolation are not claimed.

Run `node record-candidate.mjs` for the finite candidate campaign. [source-provenance.json](source-provenance.json) authenticates16 selected original source/license files from the exact1381-entry sourceZIP, and [contract-evidence.json](contract-evidence.json) binds their spans and distinguishes source study from executed helper evidence. [NOTICE.md](NOTICE.md) and the preserved GPL license retain attribution. No sourceZIP, original native executable or original DLL is supplied as an application dependency. The frozen docs/manual supplement and old compatibility records remain separate finite evidence.
